{"name":"Empire Porcelain","hex":"#e0dbd3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"empire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A political state, often a monarchy, that has achieved a much greater current size than its initial size by conquering surrounding territories, cities or nations.","A political unit ruled by an emperor or empress."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Empire."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/empire"},{"term":"porcelain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard white translucent ceramic, originally made by firing kaolin, quartz, and feldspar at high temperatures but now also inclusive of similar artificial materials; also often  such a material as a symbol of the fragility, elegance, etc. traditionally associated with porcelain goods.","Synonym of china: porcelain tableware."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To coat with a porcelain enamel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/porcelain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Empire","blocks":[{"text":"An empire is a group of territories controlled by a monarch or a supreme central authority. Within an empire, different populations may have different sets of rights and may be governed differently. The word \"empire\" derives from the Roman concept of Imperium, which refers to the right to command. Empires have taken many forms throughout history."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Modern_Empires.svg/330px-Modern_Empires.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire"},{"title":"Porcelain","blocks":[{"text":"Porcelain, also called china, is a ceramic material made by heating raw materials, generally including kaolinite, in a kiln to temperatures between 1,200 and 1,400 °C. The greater strength and translucence of porcelain, relative to other types of pottery, arise mainly from vitrification and the formation of the mineral mullite within the body at these high temperatures. End applications include tableware, decorative ware such as figurines, and products in technology and industry such as electrical insulators and laboratory ware."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/%E6%B1%9D%E7%AA%AF_%E9%9D%92%E7%93%B7%E8%86%BD%E7%93%B6.png/330px-%E6%B1%9D%E7%AA%AF_%E9%9D%92%E7%93%B7%E8%86%BD%E7%93%B6.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porcelain"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/CUH_Agesilaus_and_Pharnabazus.jpg/500px-CUH_Agesilaus_and_Pharnabazus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/CUH_Agesilaus_and_Pharnabazus.jpg/1280px-CUH_Agesilaus_and_Pharnabazus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/CUH_Agesilaus_and_Pharnabazus.jpg/500px-CUH_Agesilaus_and_Pharnabazus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meeting between Spartan king Agesilaus (left) and Pharnabazus II (right).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACUH_Agesilaus_and_Pharnabazus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Allegorie_van_de_herfst_-_onbekend_-_KU_Leuven_Kunstpatrimonium_-_SPK379.jpg/120px-Allegorie_van_de_herfst_-_onbekend_-_KU_Leuven_Kunstpatrimonium_-_SPK379.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Allegorie_van_de_herfst_-_onbekend_-_KU_Leuven_Kunstpatrimonium_-_SPK379.jpg/250px-Allegorie_van_de_herfst_-_onbekend_-_KU_Leuven_Kunstpatrimonium_-_SPK379.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons